\paragraph{Robustness: seed-level vs.\ clustered bootstrap TOST.}
The primary TOST $p$-values use seed-level paired analysis (df${=}$4), treating each training seed as the unit of replication (\S\ref{sec:setup}). Under problem-level clustered bootstrap (resampling 114 problem clusters, 10K iterations), the \cls{}--\traj{} raw TOST $p$ decreases to ${\sim}$0.007 (Holm-adjusted $p{\approx}0.021$), which would confirm equivalence. We report the more conservative seed-level result ($p{=}0.025$, adjusted $p{=}0.075$) as primary, since training seeds are the true unit of replication; problem-level bootstrap pools within-seed observations and may understate between-seed uncertainty. For \cls{}--\cwm{}, both methods yield $p{=}0.007$, so the choice does not affect conclusions.

